A||N||2||MontanaFiero||To anyone that has done a 3.4 conversion...||||1||
Z||000000||MontanaFiero||08-12-2001||04:16 AM||||I picked up a  '93 3.4 camaro motor from a salvage yard last week. The motor only has 9,000 miles on it and the cylinders were protected with atf for storage.  <P>Anyway-- The yard just happened to have a flywheel off of a <B>1988</B> Fiero 2.8L motor, which they gave me with the camaro motor. My question is this-- If the 3.4 Liter flywheel, and all of the 3.1 L flywheels have an extra "locator hole" in the flywheel where it bolts to the crank, does it matter that the 2.8L '88 flywheel they gave me <B>lacks</B> this extra hole? Did I get the correct flywheel?  Would I be safer to pick up a 3.1 flywheel?<P><B>Also</B> I would like to hear your comments and experiences from those of you that drilled your own starter holes, and any hints you may have. I have access to a jig for this purpose, but I am a little uneasy and need to be assured I can do this-- I only get one shot.<P>Here goes nuthin<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://members.home.net/blee291/fiero/mtfieroplate.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||mrfixit58||08-12-2001||12:31 PM||||The pin is there only to help you locate the correct rotation of the flywheel since the bolt holes are not spaced evenly. The motor is internally balanced and the flywheel is neutral. Just make sure all the holes line up before you tighten anything down. The 88 Fiero flywheel is actually lighter than the later models. this will translate into quicker revs. Sounds like you got a deal.<P>Roy||||reg||14
Z||000002||mrfixit58||08-12-2001||12:32 PM||||Sorry... double post.<BR>Roy<p>[This message has been edited by mrfixit58 (edited 08-12-2001).]||||reg||14
